{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>And because He loved your fathers.</b> That is to say, He caused you to hear His voice for the love of the fathers." "<b>And He chose his seed.</b> It refers back to Jacob, the third among the patriarchs. Therefore it did not say 'their seed' (בזרעם), for that would include Esau and Ishmael and the rest of the nations. Therefore it says in the periciah והיה עקב (Deuteronomy 10): ״רק באבותיך חשק ה' לאהבה אותם ויבחר בזרעם אחריהם בכם״ — only in your fathers did HaShem set His love, to love them, and He chose their seed after them — in you — because it said 'their seed' (בזרעם), it was necessary to say 'in you' (בכם), to exclude Esau and Ishmael. And it could have said 'and because He loved' (ובעבור כי אהב) or 'for the sake of' (למען), but instead it said ותחת after the manner of (Exodus 24): ״ותחת רגליו״, and these two are in the Torah, no more, alluding to what the Sages expounded: the patriarchs are the very Chariot (המרכבה)." "<b>And He brought you out in His presence.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): in His own person, after the manner of (II Samuel 17): ״ופניך הולכים בקרב״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'In His presence' (בפניו) — in His anger, after the manner of (Exodus 33): ״פני ילכו״, (I Samuel 1): ״ופניה לא היו לה עוד״, and the sense is: in the anger that He was angry against the Egyptians." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'And He brought you out in His presence' (ובפניו) — this is the angel of the Presence (מלאך הפנים), as it says (Isaiah 63): ״ומלאך פניו הושיעם״, in His great power that is within Him (שבקרבו), and understand this. Furthermore, the verse also includes the merit of the patriarchs, by whose merit they went out from Egypt: 'in His presence' (בפניו) — this is Jacob, as it is written (Psalms 24): ״מבקשי פניך יעקב סלה״; 'His great power' (הגדול) — this is Abraham. And it began from Jacob because of his preeminence, for he is the threefold cord (החוט המשולש) in the merit of the patriarchs, and the middle line (הקו האמצעי) is the principal one; and likewise in another place it begins from Jacob, in the verse (Leviticus 26): ״וזכרתי את בריתי יעקוב״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya quotes the verse from Deuteronomy 10 in a form closer to the received text than his own lemma here; the bracketed variants reflect his alternation between זרעו and זרעם as he works through the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:43.935376759Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}